    "CustomerSummary": "Customers 1-63 were affected by LPL's failure to supervise direct business transactions, which resulted in potentially unsuitable purchases of mutual fund shares. Specifically, LPL's review identified 98 purchases of class C mutual fund shares that were potentially unsuitable for customers with long investment horizons, and 74 purchases of class B mutual fund shares that were potentially inconsistent with customers' investment horizons and liquidity needs. These customers collectively paid approximately $546,000 in excessive sales charges. Individual restitution amounts ranged from $14.61 to $55,130.81, with Customer 1 having the highest restitution amount of $55,130.81. The Care Obligation violation involved LPL's failure to ensure representatives collected complete customer profile information necessary to make suitable recommendations.",
    "OverallSummary": "LPL Financial violated Regulation Best Interest's Care Obligation from May 2017 to November 2022 by failing to establish, maintain, and enforce a supervisory system reasonably designed to ensure that recommendations of Listed BDCs complied with FINRA Rule 2111 and Reg BI's Care Obligation. The case involved three main categories of violations: (1) Failure to supervise direct business transactions affecting 63 customers who paid approximately $546,000 in excessive sales charges for unsuitable mutual fund share classes; (2) Providing inaccurate information in approximately 11,300 switch letters to customers and failing to supervise switch transactions, affecting 46 customers who paid approximately $31,000 in sales charges; and (3) Failing to reasonably supervise Listed BDC transactions, affecting 16 customers who incurred $73,930 in realized losses due to overconcentration in Listed BDCs. The analytical steps used to determine violations included: (1) identifying LPL's failure to ensure representatives reported direct business transactions on trade blotters used for exception reports; (2) determining LPL failed to collect complete customer investment profile information necessary for suitability determinations; (3) identifying inaccurate information in switch letters sent to customers; and (4) determining LPL's electronic supervisory tools failed to reasonably alert supervisors to potentially unsuitable concentration levels in Listed BDCs. FINRA imposed sanctions including a $5.5 million fine and $651,374.51 in restitution plus interest."
